1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,540
You're here.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,380
Your organs are failing.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,300
And your death is drawing near.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,440
But if you bribe the right politicians in the right order at the right time,

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,600
you can trade the nuclear warheads for medicine.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,080
And it's not even a crime.

7
00:01:40,444 --> 00:01:44,444
Health Dividend Six thousand six hundred and fifty diseases have zero approved

8
00:01:44,444 --> 00:01:45,004
treatments.

9
00:01:45,544 --> 00:01:49,124
At your current pace, you'll get to all of them in four hundred and forty-three

10
00:01:49,124 --> 00:01:49,644
years.

11
00:01:50,284 --> 00:01:54,264
Redirect one percent of military spending and it drops to thirty-six years,

12
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:59,744
preventing ten point seven billion deaths at fifty thousand three hundred

13
00:01:59,744 --> 00:02:03,504
times the cost-effectiveness of anti-malaria bed nets.

14
00:02:03,984 --> 00:02:07,684
The whole thing costs less per year to run than Halloween costumes for dogs.

15
00:02:08,264 --> 00:02:09,214
But sure, take your time.

16
00:02:09,944 --> 00:02:11,604
That was the peace dividend:

17
00:02:11,664 --> 00:02:14,604
the money you get from buying one percent fewer explosions.

18
00:02:15,184 --> 00:02:17,204
Now here is the health dividend:

19
00:02:17,284 --> 00:02:19,684
what happens when you spend it on medicine instead.

20
00:02:20,604 --> 00:02:24,904
Right now, somewhere in your regulatory system, a drug that already passed

21
00:02:24,904 --> 00:02:29,084
safety testing is sitting in a filing cabinet while the disease it treats

22
00:02:29,084 --> 00:02:30,164
keeps killing people.

23
00:02:31,064 --> 00:02:35,484
Somewhere else, a researcher with a promising compound for one of six thousand

24
00:02:35,484 --> 00:02:40,304
six hundred and fifty untreated diseases is filling out the same form for

25
00:02:40,304 --> 00:02:40,924
the fourth time.

26
00:02:41,864 --> 00:02:44,704
The health dividend is what happens when you stop doing this.

27
00:02:46,124 --> 00:02:50,554
It is worth eighty-four point eight quadrillion dollars.

28
00:02:50,964 --> 00:02:52,104
That number is not a typo.

29
00:02:52,684 --> 00:02:53,344
I checked.

30
00:02:53,444 --> 00:02:54,464
Your economists checked.

31
00:02:54,984 --> 00:02:55,724
Everyone checked.

32
00:02:56,044 --> 00:02:57,164
It's still that number.

33
00:02:57,784 --> 00:02:59,624
The rest of this chapter is the receipt.

34
00:03:00,664 --> 00:03:02,964
From Explosions to Trial Slots.

35
00:03:04,204 --> 00:03:08,854
twenty-seven point two billion dollars arrives from the peace dividend.

36
00:03:09,104 --> 00:03:11,244
Investor and political allocations take their cut,

37
00:03:12,084 --> 00:03:16,064
leaving twenty-one point eight billion dollars for medical research (eighty

38
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:16,944
percent of the total).

39
00:03:17,704 --> 00:03:19,584
Your decentralized F.D.A.

40
00:03:19,584 --> 00:03:21,764
takes forty million dollars to keep the lights on.

41
00:03:22,144 --> 00:03:26,204
That leaves twenty-one point seven billion dollars for trial subsidies.

42
00:03:27,064 --> 00:03:29,304
I am now going to show you what that money buys.

43
00:03:29,904 --> 00:03:32,474
I apologize in advance for the number of numbers.

44
00:03:32,844 --> 00:03:36,444
Your species requires receipts before it will believe anything,

45
00:03:37,204 --> 00:03:38,944
even things that are obviously true.

46
00:03:39,804 --> 00:03:42,314
Especially things that are obviously true.

47
00:03:42,384 --> 00:03:46,484
Your current system charges forty-one thousand dollars per patient in a

48
00:03:46,484 --> 00:03:47,164
clinical trial.

49
00:03:47,704 --> 00:03:52,624
I asked why and was told "regulatory compliance, site management, and monitoring

50
00:03:52,624 --> 00:03:57,484
overhead," which I believe is the human phrase for "permission costs more

51
00:03:57,484 --> 00:03:58,284
than medicine."

52
00:03:58,984 --> 00:04:02,064
Pragmatic trials, the kind your decentralized F.D.A.

53
00:04:02,204 --> 00:04:04,824
runs, cost nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars.

54
00:04:05,564 --> 00:04:09,884
At that price, twenty-one point seven billion dollars buys you twenty-three

55
00:04:09,884 --> 00:04:12,784
point four million patient trial slots per year.

56
00:04:13,564 --> 00:04:14,724
Your species currently

57
00:04:15,344 --> 00:04:19,784
enrolls one point nine million patients per year in clinical trials globally.

58
00:04:20,584 --> 00:04:25,194
The treaty adds twelve point three times more capacity on top of that.

59
00:04:25,284 --> 00:04:26,084
Remember this number.

60
00:04:26,664 --> 00:04:28,244
Everything that follows depends on it.

61
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:29,844
The Waiting List.

62
00:04:31,044 --> 00:04:33,904
You met the queue in the untapped therapeutic frontier:

63
00:04:34,384 --> 00:04:39,464
six thousand six hundred and fifty diseases with zero approved treatments,

64
00:04:39,484 --> 00:04:42,164
and fifteen getting their first treatment per year.

65
00:04:42,864 --> 00:04:46,864
At that rate, you clear the backlog in four hundred and forty-three years.

66
00:04:47,524 --> 00:04:49,304
I will let you sit with that number for a moment.

67
00:04:49,844 --> 00:04:52,424
It is longer than your species has had writing.

68
00:04:53,204 --> 00:04:56,784
For context, four hundred and forty-three years ago, Shakespeare was writing

69
00:04:56,784 --> 00:05:01,684
plays, Galileo was building telescopes, and your species already had printing

70
00:05:01,684 --> 00:05:05,324
presses, universities, and intercontinental shipping.

71
00:05:05,324 --> 00:05:08,144
You just had not gotten around to curing most diseases.

72
00:05:08,644 --> 00:05:12,504
At this pace, you still will not for another four hundred and forty-three

73
00:05:12,504 --> 00:05:13,004
years.

74
00:05:13,624 --> 00:05:16,034
Apply the twelve point three times multiplier.

75
00:05:16,224 --> 00:05:19,504
Instead of fifteen diseases getting their first treatment per year, you

76
00:05:19,504 --> 00:05:22,184
get one hundred and eighty-five diseases per year.

77
00:05:22,704 --> 00:05:26,184
The queue clears in thirty-six years instead of four hundred and forty-three

78
00:05:26,184 --> 00:05:26,684
years.

79
00:05:27,064 --> 00:05:30,904
The average untreated disease gets its first treatment two hundred and four

80
00:05:30,904 --> 00:05:31,924
years earlier.

81
00:05:32,984 --> 00:05:36,384
That is the difference between "your grandchildren might see a cure" and

82
00:05:36,644 --> 00:05:37,664
"you might see a cure."

83
00:05:38,444 --> 00:05:41,724
On Wishonia, we consider this a meaningful distinction.

84
00:05:42,084 --> 00:05:46,424
On Earth, it apparently requires a seventy-eight-page chapter to justify.

85
00:05:47,284 --> 00:05:51,564
Your species figured out how to split atoms in six years during a war, but

86
00:05:51,564 --> 00:05:55,604
letting a cancer patient take a pill that passed safety testing requires

87
00:05:55,604 --> 00:05:58,904
the gestation period of three consecutive elephants.

88
00:05:59,584 --> 00:06:02,884
The twelve point three times multiplier compresses the elephant parade.

89
00:06:03,924 --> 00:06:06,624
But trial capacity is only half the delay.

90
00:06:07,204 --> 00:06:11,004
Once a drug passes safety testing, your regulatory system sits on it for

91
00:06:11,004 --> 00:06:15,064
another eight point two years to confirm it works well enough, while people

92
00:06:15,064 --> 00:06:17,314
who could be taking it die instead.

93
00:06:18,024 --> 00:06:21,704
Add the capacity acceleration and the efficacy lag together:

94
00:06:21,784 --> 00:06:23,364
two hundred and twelve years of dying,

95
00:06:24,104 --> 00:06:25,644
condensed into not dying.

96
00:06:27,104 --> 00:06:27,604
The Body Count.

97
00:06:29,064 --> 00:06:33,504
One hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day die from disease every day.

98
00:06:34,164 --> 00:06:37,324
While you were reading the previous section, approximately three hundred

99
00:06:37,324 --> 00:06:37,944
of them did.

100
00:06:38,524 --> 00:06:41,354
They are not coming back to find out how the math turned out.

101
00:06:41,744 --> 00:06:45,244
Not all of those deaths are currently preventable, but ninety-two point

102
00:06:45,424 --> 00:06:49,204
six percent are eventually avoidable with sufficient biomedical research.

103
00:06:49,304 --> 00:06:52,664
"Eventually avoidable" does not mean "currently preventable."

104
00:06:52,984 --> 00:06:53,604
It means:

105
00:06:53,684 --> 00:06:57,064
given enough research, these diseases have biological solutions.

106
00:06:57,804 --> 00:07:02,184
The only category excluded is accidents (falling off things, being hit by

107
00:07:02,184 --> 00:07:02,834
things).

108
00:07:03,124 --> 00:07:07,524
Every disease, including aging, is a biological process, and biological

109
00:07:07,524 --> 00:07:10,124
processes are, in principle, fixable.

110
00:07:10,884 --> 00:07:14,404
This is the theoretical ceiling, not a near-term promise.

111
00:07:15,284 --> 00:07:19,224
Over a two hundred and twelve-year timeline shift, the cumulative toll:

112
00:07:19,704 --> 00:07:21,384
ten point seven billion deaths.

113
00:07:22,064 --> 00:07:26,864
That is five hundred and sixty-five billion disability-adjusted life years,

114
00:07:26,964 --> 00:07:32,724
or D.A.L.Y.s, of healthy life, and one point nine three quadrillion hours

115
00:07:32,724 --> 00:07:34,124
of suffering eliminated.

116
00:07:35,044 --> 00:07:39,124
These are cumulative, one-time benefits from permanently accelerating the

117
00:07:39,124 --> 00:07:43,824
discovery pipeline, the same methodology used to value smallpox eradication.

118
00:07:44,764 --> 00:07:47,454
Nobody objected to the methodology when it was used for smallpox.

119
00:07:48,244 --> 00:07:52,624
They will object now because the number is larger, which is a strange reason

120
00:07:52,624 --> 00:07:54,224
to object to saving more people.

121
00:07:55,444 --> 00:07:57,914
The Price Tag on Not Being Dead.

122
00:07:58,444 --> 00:08:01,964
Your governments already put a price on human life when deciding whether

123
00:08:01,964 --> 00:08:05,204
to approve drugs, build highways, or regulate pollution.

124
00:08:05,904 --> 00:08:06,784
The standard rate:

125
00:08:07,064 --> 00:08:11,544
one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year.

126
00:08:13,164 --> 00:08:17,614
Your species finds this uncomfortable to discuss but does it constantly.

127
00:08:18,224 --> 00:08:21,604
You just prefer to do it in footnotes, where the dying can't see.

128
00:08:22,484 --> 00:08:27,504
The world loses two point eight eight billion D.A.L.Y.s per year.

129
00:08:28,104 --> 00:08:31,944
Ninety-two point six percent are eventually avoidable with sufficient research.

130
00:08:32,604 --> 00:08:36,204
At one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year,

131
00:08:36,584 --> 00:08:42,484
or Q.A.L.Y., that is four hundred trillion dollars per year in welfare losses.

132
00:08:43,044 --> 00:08:46,684
Multiply by the two hundred and twelve-year timeline shift and you get the

133
00:08:46,684 --> 00:08:49,154
total economic value of the health dividend:

134
00:08:49,924 --> 00:08:52,944
eighty-four point eight quadrillion dollars.

135
00:08:54,024 --> 00:08:57,584
"But that is more than global G.D.P.!"

136
00:08:57,584 --> 00:08:58,084
Yes.

137
00:08:58,444 --> 00:08:58,944
G.D.P.

138
00:08:59,324 --> 00:09:02,864
(one hundred and fifteen trillion dollars) measures market transactions.

139
00:09:03,564 --> 00:09:07,204
It does not measure the value of not being dead,

140
00:09:07,924 --> 00:09:12,664
not being in pain, and not watching your children die of treatable diseases.

141
00:09:13,724 --> 00:09:17,524
The one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year,

142
00:09:17,564 --> 00:09:18,584
or Q.A.L.Y.

143
00:09:18,804 --> 00:09:21,124
figure is not something I invented.

144
00:09:21,704 --> 00:09:25,684
I just multiplied it by the number of sick people, which is also not my

145
00:09:25,684 --> 00:09:26,184
fault.

146
00:09:27,164 --> 00:09:31,664
The only debatable input is whether the timeline shift is really approximately

147
00:09:31,664 --> 00:09:33,404
two hundred and twelve years.

148
00:09:34,404 --> 00:09:37,964
See the full derivation for how that number is calculated, and the Monte

149
00:09:37,964 --> 00:09:41,084
Carlo simulations for what happens when every input varies.

150
00:09:42,364 --> 00:09:44,184
The Receipt.

151
00:09:44,184 --> 00:09:46,304
Your decentralized F.D.A.

152
00:09:46,304 --> 00:09:48,864
costs forty million dollars a year to operate.

153
00:09:49,764 --> 00:09:54,184
For reference, this is roughly what your species spends annually on Halloween

154
00:09:54,184 --> 00:09:55,724
costumes for dogs.

155
00:09:56,524 --> 00:09:57,944
Less than one fighter jet.

156
00:09:58,584 --> 00:10:03,824
Less than what FIFA spends on "hospitality" (your word for bribing officials

157
00:10:03,824 --> 00:10:05,694
in a building with chandeliers).

158
00:10:06,504 --> 00:10:11,624
A system that could test cures for seven thousand diseases costs less to

159
00:10:11,624 --> 00:10:16,004
run than the outfits you put on animals that do not want to wear them.

160
00:10:17,124 --> 00:10:21,464
The R and D savings alone are fifty-eight point six billion dollars a year,

161
00:10:21,884 --> 00:10:26,884
a six hundred and thirty-seven to one return without counting a single human

162
00:10:26,884 --> 00:10:27,384
life.

163
00:10:28,224 --> 00:10:31,484
Include the lives (which you would think someone would, but your species

164
00:10:31,484 --> 00:10:35,414
has a long history of not counting the people who die quietly),

165
00:10:36,404 --> 00:10:41,744
and the treaty's total societal return hits eighty-four point eight million

166
00:10:41,744 --> 00:10:42,984
to one.

167
00:10:44,004 --> 00:10:46,284
The cost per year of healthy life saved:

168
00:10:47,284 --> 00:10:48,524
eighty-four cents.

169
00:10:49,664 --> 00:10:53,704
Anti-malaria bed nets, the gold standard of cost-effective interventions,

170
00:10:54,084 --> 00:10:58,484
cost eighty-nine dollars per disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y.

171
00:10:59,064 --> 00:11:04,024
This system operates at fifty thousand three hundred times the cost-effectiveness,

172
00:11:04,884 --> 00:11:06,524
at vastly greater scale.

173
00:11:07,264 --> 00:11:11,044
This costs less per life-year than mosquito nets

174
00:11:11,664 --> 00:11:13,064
and nobody has heard of it.

175
00:11:13,604 --> 00:11:17,304
Your species has spent more money marketing energy drinks.

176
00:11:18,144 --> 00:11:19,744
The total campaign investment:

177
00:11:20,544 --> 00:11:21,744
one billion dollars.

178
00:11:22,324 --> 00:11:26,844
Even the worst case (everything goes wrong, adoption is terrible, the servers

179
00:11:26,844 --> 00:11:27,504
catch fire)

180
00:11:28,404 --> 00:11:32,184
still returns an estimated sixty-six to one return.

181
00:11:32,904 --> 00:11:37,164
Your worst medical outcome is better than your best military one.

182
00:11:38,164 --> 00:11:43,024
Every day without this system costs one hundred and sixty-one million dollars

183
00:11:43,024 --> 00:11:45,804
in wasted trial spending alone.

184
00:11:46,484 --> 00:11:49,444
The regulatory lag is not a safety feature.

185
00:11:49,924 --> 00:11:52,464
It is a waiting room where people die.

186
00:11:52,904 --> 00:11:54,284
You just gave it a nicer name.

187
00:11:55,224 --> 00:11:56,584
For you personally:

188
00:11:56,604 --> 00:12:01,104
if diseases get cured even a few years earlier, the average human gains

189
00:12:01,104 --> 00:12:04,624
roughly fifty thousand extra hours of life.

190
00:12:05,444 --> 00:12:08,804
You spent seventeen minutes reading this chapter.

191
00:12:09,364 --> 00:12:14,084
That is a one hundred and seventy-six thousand times return on your time.

192
00:12:14,584 --> 00:12:17,424
Your best investment ever was reading.

193
00:12:18,244 --> 00:12:22,004
I realize the irony, given that most of you stopped reading twelve paragraphs

194
00:12:22,004 --> 00:12:22,524
ago.

195
00:12:23,204 --> 00:12:29,714
Visit impact dot war on disease dot org for derivations, Monte Carlo simulations,

196
00:12:30,224 --> 00:12:32,624
and the comparative cost-effectiveness table.

197
00:12:45,156 --> 00:12:50,696
Thank you so much for taking the time to end war and disease, but if at

198
00:12:50,696 --> 00:12:54,916
any point you decide you're too busy, please press the share button on your

199
00:12:54,916 --> 00:12:58,616
glowing rectangle in case one of your friends have a free afternoon to end

200
00:12:58,616 --> 00:12:59,656
war and disease instead.

201
00:13:00,276 --> 00:13:04,616
And if you're too busy to even do that, please leave a really, really, really

202
00:13:04,616 --> 00:13:08,776
good rating and review so we overcome the pro -war algorithmic suppression

203
00:13:08,776 --> 00:13:12,336
and find someone with more spare time for ending wars and diseases.

204
00:13:12,896 --> 00:13:15,096
The good review doesn't have to be true, they don't check.

205
00:13:15,636 --> 00:13:19,696
But if you're too busy to even do that, the absolute least you could do

206
00:13:19,696 --> 00:13:25,316
is take 15 seconds to go to WarOnDisease.org and make your wishocratic allocation,

207
00:13:25,736 --> 00:13:29,296
telling us what fraction of humanity's resources should go to explosions

208
00:13:29,296 --> 00:13:30,756
versus clinical trials.

209
00:13:31,036 --> 00:13:31,536
And vote!

210
00:13:31,956 --> 00:13:36,216
If the 1% treaty passes, your vote will be personally to blame for saving

211
00:13:36,216 --> 00:13:40,836
2.6 lives and preventing 468,000 hours of suffering.

212
00:13:40,836 --> 00:13:43,536
Go to WarOnDisease.org right now.

213
00:13:43,936 --> 00:13:44,496
Love you, bye!
